>
  <Fragment slot="verdict">
    <strong>Mango tends to run small and narrow for US shoppers.</strong> Like fellow Spanish
    brand Zara, it cuts to a slim European block with less ease through the shoulders, arms, and
    hips than American labels. Many US shoppers size up one, particularly in blazers, fitted
    dresses, and tailored pants — while relaxed and oversized styles usually fit true.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <li><strong>Borrow your Zara size:</strong> the two Spanish brands share a similar slim block, so whatever works at Zara is the best starting guess at Mango.</li>
    <li><strong>Size up in structure:</strong> blazers, sheath dresses, and tailored trousers carry the least ease — go one up if you're between sizes or layering.</li>
    <li><strong>Let relaxed cuts be relaxed:</strong> styles sold as oversized or wide-leg already include the extra room, so stay with your regular size there.</li>
    <li><strong>Convert from EU, then adjust:</strong> start with the standard EU-to-US chart below, and remember the slim cut means the converted size fits closer than a US brand's would.</li>
  </Fragment>
